markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Guiding Students through Coding Levels 

<img src="https://images.code.org/53796bf2dca42a298321692071188a94-image-1777991333557.png" alt="icon of a student with question marks" width="150px" style="border-radius:10px; float:right; margin: 0 80px 0px 20px;">

Students sometimes need support when coding with AI. Some common struggles are: 
- Skip reading the existing code
- Don’t understand the context
- Jump straight to asking AI



## Steps for success

This protocol helps set up students to uderstand, modify, and improve their code. 

<div style="width: 100%; max-width: 760px; margin: 20px 0px;">

  <div style="margin-bottom: 14px;">
    <div style="background-color: #B7E0E5; color: #000000; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 10px;">
      <p style="font-size: 18pt; font-weight: bold; margin: 0;">🔍 Understand the Starter Code</p>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size: 14pt; line-height: 1.3; margin: 8px 8px 0px 8px;">Help students first make sense of what's already there. Encourage them to identify what the code is doing before trying to change it.</p> <p style="font-size: 14pt; line-height: 1.3; margin: 8px 8px 0px 8px;">You may want to try a whole group walk through of the starter code, or encourage students to pair up and explain it to each other.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-bottom: 14px;">
    <div style="background-color: #B7E0E5; color: #000000; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 10px;">
      <p style="font-size: 18pt; font-weight: bold; margin: 0;">📌 Locate the TODO in the Comments</p>  
    </div>
    <p style="font-size: 14pt; line-height: 1.3; margin: 8px 8px 0px 8px;">Direct students to the TODO sections, which highlight exactly where changes or additions need to be made.</p><p style="font-size: 14pt; line-height: 1.3; margin: 8px 8px 0px 8px;">Guide students to point at where the code they need to add goes.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-bottom: 14px;">
    <div style="background-color: #B7E0E5; color: #000000; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 10px;">
      <p style="font-size: 18pt; font-weight: bold; margin: 0;">🧠 Plan the Behavior (in words)</p>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size: 14pt; line-height: 1.3; margin: 8px 8px 0px 8px;">Before jumping into code, have students describe what they want the program to do in plain language. This builds clarity and intention.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-bottom: 14px;">
    <div style="background-color: #B7E0E5; color: #000000; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 10px;">
      <p style="font-size: 18pt; font-weight: bold; margin: 0;">🤖 Prompt the AI</p>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size: 14pt; line-height: 1.3; margin: 8px 8px 0px 8px;">Support students in writing a clear prompt using structure: Support students in writing a clear prompt using structure (Context, Goal, Constraints, Output Expectations), so the AI can generate useful code.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-bottom: 14px;">
    <div style="background-color: #B7E0E5; color: #000000; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 10px;">
      <p style="font-size: 18pt; font-weight: bold; margin: 0;">🔁 Test and Refine</p>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size: 14pt; line-height: 1.3; margin: 8px 8px 0px 8px;">Encourage students to run their code, check if it works, and make improvements. Iteration is key—students should expect to adjust both their code and their prompts.</p>
  </div>

</div>

> #### As you work through these coding levels, practice using these steps yourself. Notice how clear planning and prompting improve the AI’s responses, and think about how you will model and scaffold this process for your students.
MARKDOWN
